00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.610 align:start position:0%
 
I've been waiting for an opportunity to  rerun this week's classic interview it's  with Anthony Papa and I interviewed him  back in 2015 and he's from the drug  policy alliance and he's actually a  victim of extreme drug sentencing and  this is so important because we are  talking a lot lately about these pardons  from President Trump we talked about the  pardoning of Alice Marie Johnson we've  talked about the possibility of  pardoning others in the future but also  what seemed to be sort of like political  or optics or wasted pardons from Donald  Trump and so in reviewing this interview  with Anthony Pappa what I want to think  about as we listen to it is when we  prioritize pardons what should we be  thinking of what should the principles  and priorities be and with that let's  check out my interview from October of  2015 with Anthony Papa I'm joined today  by Anthony Papa whose manager of media  relations for the drug policy Alliance  he also has personal experience with the  criminal justice system and the war on  drugs he was sentenced to 15 years to  life and ultimately served 12 years in  sing-sing Anthony it's great to have you  let's start for people who may not be  familiar with your story talk to us a  little bit about your conviction thanks  for having me on your show it was the  first time nonviolent drug offender in  1985 I made the biggest mistake of my  life I got involved with drug activity a  person a bold on my bowling team asked  me why I was coming late to legal the  time and I told Mike my car keeps  breaking down he said why don't you fix  it I didn't have any money well from  that point on he introduced me to  someone who was dealing drugs in the  bowling alleys in Westchester County he  asked me if I wanted to make a fast  dollar I said for what he says deliver  an envelope from the Bronx to Mount  Vernon and I'll give you five hundred  dollars at first I said no because I  knew it was about and I passed it passed  it by a couple of months later it was  Christmastime things got desperate I  needed money and he asked me again nice  I'll do it so I wanted to bring in this  envelope containing four and a half  ounces of cocaine up from the Bronx to  Mount Vernon but I walked into a police  sting operation 20 cops came out of  nowhere was placed under arrest and I  did everything I could do wrong and  eventually I was sentenced to 15 years  to life under the Rockefeller drug laws  which mandates mandatory minimum  sentencing and were you did this go to  trial or did you take a plea deal no I  took a plea they actually offered me  three to life because they knew I wasn't  a drug dealer but I didn't want to leave  my wife and my seven-year-old daughter  and I went to trial and it was a big  mistake because I got walloped with to  15 of life sentences one for sales one  for possession of the four and a half  ounces of cocaine but since that was the  first time nonviolent drug offender the  judge gave me a break ran the sentence  together so I only had 115 a life  sentence I served at st. st.  correctional facility and talk to us a  little bit about that I mean were you  what what types of other offenders  were you imprisoned with was it was it  similar to the crime you had been  convicted over was a drastically  different violent crime etc it was it  was a mixed bag I mean you know  everybody I went to sing sings a  maximum-security prison so you you're  sentenced along with others that has  sentenced for murder rape I mean just  heinous crime and it was a nightmare  experience to say the least you know  when I was in prison I was lost I didn't  know what to do I was my identity who I  was in life and became prisoner number  85 80 20 30 7 what saved my life was my  discovery of art we're through another  prisoner I learned that I was an artist  prison is a very existential environment  and what I mean by that is there's  something mystical about being in prison  in a 6x9 cage for many years you find  out about yourself so for me I found out  that I was an artist  and three years later 1988 I was sitting  in my cell  picked up a mirror looked in the mirror  I saw an individual's gonna spend the  most productive years his life in a cage  and I painted the self-portrait  tiredly 15 to life seven years later  wound up with the Whitney Museum of  American Art while I was in prison I got  a lot of publicity on my case and  Governor George Pataki granted me  executive clemency and while you were  while you were incarcerated you also did  many things that that other inmates  don't do right I mean you earn two  bachelor's degree you got a masters  degree you as you mentioned you you had  George Pataki grant you clemency after  you were released you did some acting  you are on HBO's Oz you've been in  several films but even with all that  notwithstanding it you you still have  have been at the mercy of the challenges  that exist for for formerly incarcerated  persons right I mean talk about that a  little bit yeah I mean it was a tough  experience you know it's a rude  awakening  and you spend an enormous amount of time  in prison and then suddenly you're free  like these people coming out now there's  about 6,000 that are coming out under  these new changes in the law right and  they're about to enter society and  they're in for a rude awakening  it's tough every step of the way for an  ex-offender you're blocked with passing  employment it's just it's hard for  ex-offenders you're you're labeled an  ex-offender and you wear that label for  the rest of your life for me it's my 18  year of freedom and I still feel the  stigma of being an ex-offender  I just wrote about it a new book I  actually had to address this issue of  people coming home it's called men and  women returning home in an age of reform  it's available  Kindle on Amazon and basically what I  try to do is use my stories and are  telling men and women who were coming  home about my experiences because prison  doesn't end at the prison wall goes  beyond the wall it affects families  loved ones those on the outside and one  thing people won't realize is that when  you come into the free world from prison  you drag along with you all the baggage  that you use to survive imprisonment all  the coping mechanisms and you bring them  with you in the free world and at times  they suddenly appear and you have no  control over them and it's a very  dangerous situation which when this  occurs look for it a little bit about  that I mean in thinking of sort of the  broader problems we have we have the  sort of status quo of the war on drugs  we have mandatory minimums we have in in  many cases not in your particular case  but we have plea deals and the sort of  plea deal structure that is set up to to  really hurt a lot of nonviolent  offenders you have the sort of punitive  nature of incarceration as opposed to  rehabilitative where what's the what's  the top priority or in other words  what's the lowest hanging fruit that  could be tackled first that would have  the most impact  you mean when when when people are  returning to the free world just in  general in terms of reform what what are  the first changes that might have the  big impact is it talking about mandatory  minimums is it about what it what the  paesan's are like etc I think that  mandatory minimums have become a poison  that has destroyed our criminal justice  system  President Obama is speaking about this  and there's legislation now that exists  actually bipartisan legislation which  has passed the Senate waiting for  Congress to act on it which would reduce  mandatory minimum sentencing what  mandatory minimum sentence he does it  takes away the discretion of the judge  look at the totality of facts so for  example in my case I got 15 years to  life as a first-time nonviolent drug  offender that didn't matter what  mattered was the weight involved of the  drug the fauna have ounces of cocaine so  under the Rockefeller drug laws which  mandate mandatory minimum sentencing I  passed this envelope with four and a  half ounces two ounce sale or four ounce  possession Matan I got arrested would  give you a 15 to life sentence or better  men and women have received these  sentences 15 20 25 years mandatory  minimum sentencing and these laws have  become under the federal sentencing  these were the original laws in 1973 one  reenacted of the Rockefeller drug laws  it eventually became mandatory minimum  sentencing laws in the federal  government where the Act every state had  some sort of mandatory minimum  sentencing law this bill those laws that  destroyed our system it has increased  prisons to the capacity where the  prisons are exploding and it seems now  because of it became a monetary issue  everybody politicians from the left from  the right are getting together and  realizing that it's it's not  cost-efficient these laws it's a waste  of tax dollars a waste of human lives  money could be better spent on needy  communities to put shoes on streamers  children so politicians are not I'm not  acting on this issue and with the lead  from President Obama  hopefully mass incarceration will end  all right we've been speaking with  Anthony papa  I'll remind you the new book is an open  letter to men and women returning home  in an age of reform he's with the drug  policy Alliance thanks so much for  sharing your story with us yeah if you  want to know more about our organization  is drug policy org or you could go to my  website 15 to life  dot-com and see my art and my story  thanks very much for having me as a  guest

